<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tongatapu</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tongatapu is the main island of Tonga, in the southern part of the kingdom, and holds most of its people and the capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nukualofa">Nukuʻalofa</a>. A low coral island around a shallow lagoon, it was the seat of the Tuʻi Tonga kings, whose royal tombs stand at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mua-tonga">Lapaha</a> near Muʻa, and it has the trilithon of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haamonga-a-maui">Haʻamonga ʻa Maui</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-tupou-i">George Tupou I</a> made Nukuʻalofa his capital in the 19th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tongatapu.jpg" alt="Tongatapu" width="320" height="266" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: European Space Agency · CC BY-SA 3.0 igo · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tonga_ESA363260.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Tongatapu covers about 260 square kilometres and lies in Tonga's southern island group, to which it gives its name. It is built of coral limestone and is rather flat, covered with fertile soil made of volcanic ash from neighbouring volcanoes. On the steep southern coast the land rises to an average of 35 metres and at most 70 metres, then falls gradually towards the north.</p><p>To the north lie many small islands and coral reefs, reaching up to 7 kilometres from the shore. The almost closed Fanga'uta and Fangakakau lagoons, fringed with mangroves, are breeding grounds for birds and fish, and were declared a natural reserve in 1974. As the southernmost island group of the country, it has a cooler climate than the north, so fruit production is lower there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Tongatapu holds one of the densest concentrations of archaeological remains in the Pacific. The earliest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lapita-pottery">Lapita pottery</a> found in Tonga dates from about 900 to 850 BCE, around 300 years after the first settlements. The archaeologist David Burley found it near the Fanga'uta Lagoon, some 2,000 kilometres from the Lapita pottery of Santa Cruz in the Solomon Islands. Nukuleka is possibly the site of the first Lapita settlement.</p><p>The island was the seat of the Tuʻi Tonga, whose rule over distances of up to 1,000 kilometres was often only symbolic. Their first capital was at Toloa, about 1,000 years ago, and the second at Heketā, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haamonga-a-maui">Haʻamonga ʻa Maui</a> trilithon stands. The third, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mua-tonga">Muʻa</a>, was a capital from 1220 to 1851 and has more than 20 royal grave mounds.</p><p>Abel Tasman, commanding the Heemskerck and the Zeehaen for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a>, sighted the island on 20 January 1643 and named it Amsterdam Island for its abundant supplies. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-cook">James Cook</a> visited later and wrote the name as Tongataboo in 1777. British and American whalers called regularly for provisions, water and wood between 1807 and 1899.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Tongatapu is the most populous island of Tonga, with 74,611 residents in 2016, roughly 70.5% of the national total. It holds the capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nukualofa">Nukuʻalofa</a>, and has become the country's business, trade and labour centre. Its sites include the royal tombs at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mua-tonga">Muʻa</a>, the blowholes at Mapu ʻa Vaea, the natural land bridge at Hūfangalupe and Tupou College, the first educational establishment in the kingdom.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tongatapu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He gained control of Vavaʻu in 1833, and when the Tuʻi Kanokupolu Aleamotuʻa died in November 1845 he succeeded to the title on 4 December as King George Tupou I, making Nukuʻalofa on <b>Tongatapu</b> his capital.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-tonga">Kingdom of Tonga · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The early kings are remembered as living in eastern <b>Tongatapu</b>, at Heketā, where tradition credits the eleventh Tuʻi Tonga, Tuʻitātui, with raising the stone trilithon known as the Haʻamonga ʻa Maui around 1200.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tu-i-tonga-empire">Tuʻi Tonga Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Pieces of the same pottery had been found long before, by Father Otto Meyer, a Catholic missionary, on Watom Island off New Britain in 1909, and by William C. McKern on <b>Tongatapu</b> in Tonga in 1920.</blockquote><a href="/culture/lapita-culture">Lapita culture · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
